Yes. A story can function as a behavioral bootstrap.

The hardest part of many social behaviors is not knowing whether something is possible, admirable, permitted, or worth the social cost. A compelling character answers those questions in advance. They become the simulated first mover.

If the audience empathizes with the character, they partially run that character’s state internally: What would I feel? What would I do? If they also admire the character, the simulation gains positive valence: I might want to be like that.

So the mechanism is roughly:

\text{character} \rightarrow \text{empathy} \rightarrow \text{identification}
\rightarrow \text{simulated action} \rightarrow \text{reduced novelty/risk}
\rightarrow \text{imitation}

“Cool” matters because it turns behavior into an identity affordance. The audience isn’t merely being told, “Do X.” They’re shown, “A person you would willingly resemble does X.”

That bypasses one of persuasion’s biggest obstacles: the audience does not have to invent the behavioral template themselves.

A character can therefore serve several functions at once: first mover, first follower, proof-of-concept, social permission, rehearsal environment, and compressed policy demonstration.

And once somebody in the real world imitates the character, something interesting happens:

\text{fictional precedent}
\rightarrow \text{real adopter}
\rightarrow \text{observable precedent}
\rightarrow \text{social proof}
\rightarrow \text{additional adopters}

The story has effectively seeded reality with an executable example.

That’s why protagonists, saints, superheroes, rebels, founders, soldiers, celebrities, even mascots can have effects disproportionate to the literal information contained in their stories. They are not merely conveying propositions. They are providing agents people can temporarily inhabit.

The important distinction is that this can be used constructively or manipulatively. The same machinery can normalize courage, generosity, and experimentation—or manufacture prestige around destructive behavior. The underlying technology is identification-driven behavioral modeling.